# SRG-APP-000176-AU-002640  -  CAT I
# The Central Log Server, when using PKI-based authentication, must enforce
# authorized access to the corresponding private key.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000176-AU-002640
group:           V-206479
severity:        high
cat:             CAT I
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-000186
control_rev5:    IA-5

contribution:    Splunk can hold the private key at a site-managed path and protect it with
                 a password that is itself kept out of cleartext in the configuration, so
                 the key is usable only by the service that holds the password rather than
                 by anyone who can read the conf layer.

governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           server.conf [sslConfig] sslPassword not_equals password
check:           web.conf [settings] privKeyPath non_empty
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          server.conf [sslConfig] sslPassword https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] privKeyPath https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/web.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        File system ownership and permissions on the private key file and on
                 splunk.secret, which are operating system controls
external:        Custody of the key and the designated PKI Sponsor's authorization, which
                 are procedural
external:        web.conf.spec:93-99 notes that an encrypted web private key requires
                 splunkd client authentication to be off, so encrypting that key is a
                 deployment trade-off against the client certificate requirement assessed
                 under SRG-APP-000149-AU-002280
external:        Not Applicable where PKI-based authentication is not used

citation:        SRG-APP-000176-AU-002640 check text (Not Applicable where PKI-based
                 authentication is not used); server.conf.spec:989-991 (sslPassword, the
                 password for the server certificate, documented default "password"),
                 web.conf.spec:75-80 (privKeyPath, default is the auto-generated key),
                 web.conf.spec:93-99 (web.conf sslPassword protects the key named by
                 privKeyPath; with an encrypted key, splunkd client authentication must be
                 off), server.conf.spec:547-560 (encrypt_fields keeps sslPassword entries
                 stored encrypted)
finding:         server.conf [sslConfig] sslPassword is left at the documented default
                 value, or web.conf privKeyPath does not name a site-managed key, so the
                 application does not restrict use of the private key to the extent the
                 product allows.
